643304467,-0.511873185634613,-0.35471799969673157,-5.781483559985645e-05,-1.3063175678253174,-0.999260663986206,-0.9594719409942627,-0.00272973976098001,-0.16272808611392975,-0.4637906551361084,-0.00044979469384998083,-0.003147174371406436,-0.009502182714641094,-0.00019012074335478246,-1.1920922133867862e-06,-0.333512544631958,-0.5241861343383789,-0.032673727720975876,-0.09908730536699295,-0.05286206677556038,0.0,-3.099436753473128e-06,-0.022703364491462708,-0.582554817199707,-2.145764938177308e-06,-0.5916163325309753,-0.12433204054832458,-0.00658935122191906,-4.291525328881107e-06,-0.0001012035645544529,-7.152531907195225e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,26107,47,2075,11730,17,19,87,47822,17,19,92,41715,1242,15159,73,28,16,92,47822,17,18,25547,17,19,13333,10699,2359,2075,47822,17,19,13333,92,10,87,47822,17,19,28735,11035,1291,568,14085,10061,400,89,62,16,11,1147,62,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,1147,1710,3,387,279,12460,17516,315,400,47,2075,15087,11,323,1077,400,89,4698,61,17,24239,4698,35093,15159,74,92,72,3,369,400,74,28,16,11,220,17,11,1124,507,2412,11,435,54876,1380,400,72,34433,26888,19999,16,31716,11,323,400,64,4698,3,323,400,65,4698,3,525,1931,5109,13,7379,198,59,26056,1242,15159,74,28,16,92,47822,81,52398,65,4698,91,7110,60,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $$P(x)=24x^{24}+\\sum_{j=1}^{23}(24-j)\\left(x^{24-j}+x^{24+j}\\right).$$Let $z_1, z_2, \\ldots, z_r$ be the distinct zeros of $P(x)$, and let $z_k^2=a_k+b_{k}i$ for $k=1, 2, \\ldots, r$, where $i=\\sqrt{-1}$, and $a_k$ and $b_k$ are real numbers. Find\n\\[\\sum_{k=1}^{r}|b_k|.\\]","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"6e59f3abed445e65fd2d8bc0a6d3555b1672293de030c3fa08c20b4900627ecf"},"seconds":63.063305139541626,"seed":1086089236652491650,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to analyze the polynomial \\( P(x) \\) and its roots. The given polynomial is:\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) \\left( x^{24-j} + x^{24+j} \\right).\n\\]\n\nFirst, let's rewrite \\( P(x) \\) in a more convenient form:\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24+j}.\n\\]\n\nNotice that the terms \\( x^{24+j} \\) for \\( j = 1, 2, \\ldots, 23 \\) can be rewritten using \\( x^{24} \\) as:\n\\[\nx^{24+j} = x^{24} x^j.\n\\]\nThus, the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24} x^j.\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\( x^{24} \\) from the second sum:\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + x^{24} \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^j.\n\\]\n\nLet's combine the sums:\n\\[\nP(x) = 24x^{24} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{24-j} + x^{24} \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^j.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify further, let's consider the polynomial in terms of \\( x^{24} \\):\n\\[\nP(x) = x^{24} \\left( 24 + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^j \\right).\n\\]\n\nLet \\( Q(x) = 24 + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^j \\). Then:\n\\[\nP(x) = x^{24} Q(x).\n\\]\n\nThe roots of \\( P(x) \\) are the roots of \\( Q(x) \\) and the roots of \\( x^{24} = 0 \\), which are \\( x = 0 \\) with multiplicity 24. Since \\( x = 0 \\) does not contribute to the imaginary parts, we focus on the roots of \\( Q(x) \\).\n\nNext, we analyze \\( Q(x) \\):\n\\[\nQ(x) = 24 + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^{-j} + \\sum_{j=1}^{23} (24-j) x^j.\n\\]\n\nNotice that \\( Q(x) \\) is symmetric about \\( x = 0 \\). Therefore, if \\( z \\) is a root, then \\( \\frac{1}{z} \\) is also a root. This symmetry implies that the roots come in reciprocal pairs.\n\nSince \\( Q(x) \\) is a polynomial of degree 46 (as the highest power is \\( x^{46} \\) from \\( x^{-1} \\cdot x^{47} \\)), it has 46 roots. These roots are symmetric about the unit circle in the complex plane.\n\nGiven the symmetry, the imaginary parts of the roots \\( z_k \\) will come in pairs \\( (a + bi, a - bi) \\). Therefore, the sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots is:\n\\[\n\\sum_{k=1}^{r} |b_k| = 2 \\sum_{k=1}^{23} b_k.\n\\]\n\nSince the polynomial is symmetric and all roots are on the unit circle, each pair contributes \\( 2b_k \\). The sum of the absolute values of the imaginary parts of the roots is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{46}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{46}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
